            "text": "An efficient implementation in Python to compute the sum of Euclidean distances over all ordered pairs of rows.\n\n### Method Explanation\nThe array has dimensions $8000 \\times 1024$. Computing pairwise differences $(x_i - x_j)$ elementwise across all $8000^2$ pairs requires tens of billions of operations and large memory allocations that cause execution timeouts.\n\nInstead, we express the squared Euclidean distance using the algebraic identity:\n$$\\|x_i - x_j\\|_2^2 = \\|x_i\\|_2^2 + \\|x_j\\|_2^2 - 2 \\langle x_i, x_j \\rangle$$\n\nBy converting the vectors to `float64` for full precision, the pairwise inner products $\\langle x_i, x_j \\rangle$ are computed via a single BLAS-accelerated matrix multiplication ($X X^T$), which finishes in ~1 second. In-place operations are used to avoid unnecessary memory overhead.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n\ndef main():\n    # Load vectors (8000 x 1024, float32)\n    vectors = np.load(\"vectors_secondary.npy\")\n\n    # Upcast to float64 for numerical precision\n    X = vectors.astype(np.float64)\n\n    # Compute squared norms for each vector: shape (8000,)\n    norms = np.sum(X**2, axis=1)\n\n    # Compute Gram matrix G = X @ X.T using optimized BLAS dgemm\n    G = X @ X.T\n\n    # Compute pairwise squared distance: ||u - v||^2 = ||u||^2 + ||v||^2 - 2 * (u . v)\n    # Perform in-place operations on G to conserve memory\n    G *= -2.0\n    G += norms[:, None]\n    G += norms[None, :]\n\n    # Clean numerical inaccuracies and guarantee zero diagonal\n    np.fill_diagonal(G, 0.0)\n    np.maximum(G, 0.0, out=G)\n\n    # Compute Euclidean distance\n    np.sqrt(G, out=G)\n\n    total = float(np.sum(G))\n    print(f\"TOTAL:{total}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
